I love polearms.  I pretty much always carry one.

Reasons:
1. Ranged attack.
2. Ranged attack that never runs out of ammunition.
3. Can attack things that are not in a straight line at range when I
have acheived "skilled" or higher.
4. Unlike daggers, can't be scooped up by the other side and used back
on me.
5. Can attack  *THROUGH* monsters.  E.g., imagine this setup:

------
|@hL
-----

Where "h'" is  a hostile dwarf, and "L" is an arch lich.

I can a)pply the polearm to hit the lich,  going right past the dwarf.
But the lich is out of melee range and can't hit me.  Meanwhile the
dwarf will be swinging away,  but his hits are mosquito bites compared
to touch-of-death or destroy armor.

There are downsides, however:
1)  Great at 2-square range, not good at melee range.
2)  Need light to see second square
3)  Adds 50-100 units of extra weight, depending on the type you're
carrying.


Some tips to effective use:

1. Wait until you've advanced a few levels to start practicing with it.
This will obviate the "miss always" problem.

2. When practicing, aim at human size targets or larger
(goblins,dwarves, mumaks).  Small things like killer bees are much
harder to hit.

3. Keep a light source around.

4. Learn to keep monsters at distance.

For my part, I tend to carry a +0 halberd around for dealing with
disenchanters -- That way I don't lose the power on my +X Daggers of
Death, and I can still keep enough distance that I won't lose stuff on
my +X armor. 

